# Examples that need a concurrent writer or a live file rotation, so a batch replay
# can't reproduce them. Each was verified interactively in the sandbox when written
# (background writer appending to the file while tail -f/-F watched it).
Follow a file in real time
Filter a live-followed log for just the lines you care about
Plain -f keeps watching the old file after rotation
-F notices the replacement and follows the new file

# "Stop following once a specific process exits" belongs with those four and was verified
# the same way, but it documents no `output:` block, so nothing replays it and listing it
# here would exempt an example the harness never had. Named in a comment instead.
